

               MICROBEE HARDDISK STARNET 
                    PRACTICAL NOTES

USING THE STARNET NETWORK
-------------------------

The Starnet is very similar to a large computer network found  in 
the  workplace.   Very  few  businesses can afford  to  buy  each 
employee  a computer of their own.  Instead,  they all share one.  
This  saves  lots  of time and  money,  as  everyone  shares  the 
expensive Disk Drive units,  programs,  and data.  For this to be 
practical  a  great  deal of security has to be  built  into  the 
system.   Each  person using the system has to be segrigated from 
the others to keep their work safe.

The easiest way to understand this is to think of the Bank Teller 
Machines.   When you use the Bank's computer they have to be very 
careful that you can only access your own money.  They do this by 
restricting you to your own account.   Firstly,  the bank  issues 
you with an account (you open your dealings with it by sticking a 
plastic  card  into the machine) and entering  a  password  (some 
banks issue numeric passwords called PIN numbers,  others let you 
use  words).   ONLY,  after  you prove your identity by doing  it 
correctly do you have access to your accounts.

The  Starnet  works the same way.   To secure the system we  have 
given no Workstation permission to change the information on  the 
Hard disk.  The disk comes divided into 4 different drives called 
A,B,C and D.   The Floppy drive is called the E drive.  These are 
called the Public drives,  and can only be changed by the  Master.  
(You  do  have the ability to give the Workstations permission  to 
the  Public  areas,   however,  under  normal  circumstances  you 
wouldn't.)   Of  course  the  Workstations  can  access  or  load 
anything  on ABCD or E  ......  but .....  they can never  change 
anything by saving or deleting etc.

If you want to SAVE any work,  or PRINT out your work,  you  must 
first  be LOGGED IN to your account - called a WORKSPACE .   This 
gives you your own little piece of the disk called the P drive (P 
for PERSONAL).   Within your workspace you have total freedom  to 
save, erase, print, or whatever.

So, before you start a lesson think of all the things you plan to 
do  in  that lesson.   If you just want to show  the  children  a 
program  you can just go straight into it.   If you want them  to 
save their work, to finish later, or print it out, you MUST START 
OFF BY CREATING SOME WORKSPACES AND THEN HAVE THEM LOG INTO  THEM 
FIRST.  THEN LOAD THE PROGRAM.

Let's use Wordstar as an example.   You intend to do some process 
writing  with the children.    Before the lesson go to the master 
station and select

          D - Diskette/Workspace management

then      W - Create or change a Workspace


The  screen will fill with the directory and in the second bottom 
section  you  will see the cursor after  the  question  Workspace 
name:     I would suggest leaving the A drive for only commercial 
software  and  keeping  your  Workspaces on  B,C  or  D.   It  is 
important  then  to use the BACKSPACE  key to move back over  the 
letter  "A" and type a B over it.   Then type in the name of  the 
first Workspace.   Start out with your own surname,  (but keep it 
to a maximum of 8 letters).  Forget about anything after the full 
stop  - it  is too much of a nuisance to type it  in  each  time.  
Then press the RETURN key.

It will see it as a new Workspace.   If you want to change an old 
one  you  just  use  the same proceedure - except  that  it  will 
realise that it already exists and display whatever it has on it.

Type  in a password that you will be able  to  remember.   Notice 
that  the  system  will allocate 32K of space.   If this  is  not 
adequate move down and increase it.  The manual will explain too, 
that  once it is created you can never decrease it only  increase 
it otherwise you may corrupt the information stored inside it.

When you are happy type the RETURN key and a Y to confirm.

Now  you will need to RESET the Workstations to access  this  new 
information.  Then select 2 - Network functions
                          1 - Log in/out workspace

Remember it is            P for personal  (RETURN)
Then the name          eg FRED   (RETURN)
and the password          SECRET (RETURN)

Now  that they are logged in they will need to hold the ESC   key 
to go back to the main shell screen.

The select   1 - Application Programs

             1 - Wordprocessing


You  can  see  there are half a dozen steps involved  and  it  is 
possible for them to forget to log in first.   They would type up 
all their work and right at the end when they tried to save it it 
would tell them that the don't have permission to do so.

To save any heart ache we have saved this chain of events as a
"USER  DEFINED" key.   Go back to the master and I will show you 
where and how we have done it.

Select    C - Configuration
          W - Workspace Shell Configuration
          U - Default User Command Definition




The screen will fill with the numbers 0 - 9 and some rather funny 
looking commands following each one.  To explain - let's take the 
number  1  for example.   It will firstly  put  the  workstations 
screen  into the shell (sh) - this is what normally happens  when 
you  turn  a workstation on.   Instead of just stopping there  it 
will  then automatically move on to LOGIN and force the child  to 
log in to their P drive.   Once done, it will then go back to the 
A drive whrer it will go on and load the Wordstar program.  

Try  it  out on a workstation.   To activate the User  keys  just 
select  a  number 4   -  then the 1  for  Wordstar.   Follow  the 
screen  by  typing  in a Workspace name and  password.   See  how 
foolproof it is. 

Look down those User defined keys and you will see that 

0 = Graphic Wordbee
1 = Wordstar        -    Wordprocessing
2 = Multiplan       -    Spreadsheet
3 = SimplyDB        -    Database
4 = Telcom          -    Telecommunications
5 = Basic           -    Microbee's own Basic
6 = Logo+           -    Premium Logo
7 = Simply Write    -    Our latest Wordprocessor
8 = Screen Dump     -    To print out screens 
9 = Microsoft Basic -    The Basic written by Microsoft

We  have  deliberately used the same order as the  programs  were 
stored under  1 - Application Programs

Now  you  have a choise of using our short-cut User keys  if  you 
want.

If  you  prefer  to use Simply Write as the  usual  wordprocessor 
instead of Wordstar just reverse the commands in 1 and 7 and also 
change  the  Configuration   - Application Programs  to  read  WP 
instead of WS on the master.

Likewise you may prefer to have Microsoft Basic as the Basic most 
often taught in your school over Microbee's own Microworld basic.  
Just  swap  the  commands  for  No 5 and  9  and  again  go  into 
configuration  on the Master and change Application program no  5 
to read MBASIC instead of the Basic that is there.

 
USING GRAPHIC WORDBEE ON THE HARD DISK SYSTEM
---------------------------------------------

To  log the students in and then load Graphic Wordbee all they do 
is select           4 - User Commands
then                0 - Wordbee   (RETURN) 




You  can  use  Graphic  Wordbee  as  usual.    Loading  alternate 
lettering  styles  (FONTS) by selecting G  - Load  Graphics,  and 
choosing any font in the system.  You can tell a font by the .FON 
extension at the end of the file name.

SAVING

Remember,  before you try to save your work you need to move back 
to the P: drive.  Select     C - Change Disk
                             P - to pick your personal drive

Now the system will find your workspace.   If you want to load  a 
new graphic font,  file or anything that is stored on the A drive 
you will have to remember to change back to it first.

PRINTING

When you want to print out your work you just select 

                    P - Print    (as normal)

The  file  will be sent down to your spool file.   When  you  are 
completely finished you just select

                    E - Exit   are you sure?   Y

The system will take you back to the Workstation Shell.

Select              2 - Network functions

Notice  that  under  the section marked Printer:  you  have  some 
spooled data:       eg 2K

                    2 - Print spooled data

The printer will activate.

USING WORDSTAR ON THE STARNET HARD DISK SYSTEM
---------------------------------------------- 

THEY MUST LOG INTO THE WORKSPACE FIRST.

You can have them log in and then load Wordstar, or remember they 
can just pick       4 - User keys
                    1 - Wordstar

This will force them to log in and then load Word Star for them.

The  very first thing they must do is to select 

                    L - change logged drive
                    P: - to let it know P is where to save to.

They would then go on and  D - Open a Document
Give their story a name and start work.

Follow  the  Wordstar Manual for  instructions,  within  wordstar 
itself there are no changes.     

When they are finished typing, and whish to print their work they 
must save it first.  Use CTRL KD.  We are now back at the OPENING 
MENU.   To  print it out  pick the P option from this front menu.  
Notice  nothing prints out.   Infact,  it has printed it onto the 
disks  printer buffer.   It is now waiting for you to "spool"  it 
down to the printer.   (This allows the operating system to  keep 
track  of all the printer requests and make sure that only one is 
sent to the printer at a time,  and not get all the work  muddled 
up.)


Now lets print it.
    
Type  R -  Run A Program ( from the  SYSTEM COMMAND  section  of 
the OPENING MENU.)

It will ask you.....               COMMAND?    
TYPE.....                          SH PRINT

It  will then say               Hit any key to return to Wordstar.

It will too.

TO EXIT BACK TO THE WORKSTATION SHELL.
-------------------------------------
Choose                   X  exit to system.

This takes you back to CP/M itself.

Type                     sh   to get your workspace shell back.

Note.  You're still logged in, but no longer in Wordstar.




SETTING UP WORD STAR FILES SO EVERYONE CAN USE THEM.
----------------------------------------------------

Lets  say you want to type up a story with lots of  spelling  and 
grammar mistakes for your class to correct.  To do this you  will 
need to have Write Permission to a public area of the disk.  

I would recommend that you do this first before you start typing, 
(to save time.)  If you decided after the work was done  (eg if a 
student  wrote  something  brilliant that the whole  class  could 
benefit from using) don`t panic. Just have them save their work,
then  exit  out  of Wordstar,  change the  write  permissions  as 
follows and  then copy it over.   

Log into a workspace from a Workstation.

Go to the master File Server and choose

                         S    Statistics  (this will let you see
                               which Workstation Number you are.)

then                     C    Configuration
then                     A    Access permission
then                     W    Write permissions

Using  Ctrl E X S D move over to your Workspace and give yourself 
permission to either drive A,B,C or D. (using Y for yes).

Note.  Only one workstation can have write permission to a public 
drive at a time.

On the Workstation select

                         4  Request /release write permission.
It  will  ask  which  drive  (select the one  you  gave  yourself 
permission for)

Type the ESC key to go back to the main workstation menu.

Create you Wordstar Document as above (within your workspace).
Save the finished work using Ctrl KD.  

Select                   O  Copy a file (from the Opening Menu)
It asks                  Name of a file to copy from?
Type                     your file name
It asks                  Name of file to copy to?

Type the drive name and then the name of the file.
EG                       a:name.doc     Hit return


All  the  other workstations are now free to load this  file  and 
save their corrected versions to their own workspaces.

NB. Remember to turn off your write permission when you`re finished


USING MULTIPLAN ON THE STAR NETWORK
-----------------------------------                         

Multiplan  is  a program that uses multiple files to  run.   This 
means for Multiplan to work you need to tell the network where  to 
look for these additional files .

This is all taken care for you under those User Defined keys.

All  your  students  have to do now, whenever  they  want  to  use 
multiplan, is to hold the reset key.


Select                        4   User commands
                              2   Multiplan

then hit the return key

It will stop to force them to login,  then automatically continue 
on to load multiplan.

Once  in Multiplan the students will need to let the system  know 
where their workspace resides.  


Select                        T  Transfer
                              O  Options

Tab across to Setup and type  P:    

The  computer now knows that it will find its Multiplan files  on 
the  A drive,  but,   is to save its work to the P drive.   (your 
workspace).

TO PRINT MULTIPLAN
------------------

Complete your work session.

Select                   P  print
                         P  printer
it then displays         Print on the printer

(  NB   Like  wordstar  this is just putting it  into  the  print 
queue......  we will need to exit right out of multiplan to  send 
the file down to the printer).

BEFORE WE DO THIS MAKE SURE YOU SAVE YOUR WORK.
Use                           T  transfer  
                              S  save


Now                      Q quit  (you are now out in CP/M)

You then type            sh print

USING TELCOM ON THE STARNET
---------------------------

When  you  have  loaded  Telcom you will  be  confronted  with  a 
Command?  question.   Before you can save any files  you need  to 
type  a P:  (RETURN).   Now any files you receive over  Telephone 
Modem will be safely saved in your workspace.

To  print them out you will have to leave Telcom by typing either 
CPM  or CTRL C to go back to the Workspace Shell.  
Then select              5 - File Manager
                         4 - print file



type in the name you gave to your file and (RETURN)
This will send the file to the Spool file.   Now type the ESC key 
twice to move back to the first set of options and then select
                         2 - Network functions
                         2 - Print spooled data


The printer will activate.




USING BASIC ON THE STARNET
--------------------------


If  you  just want to play a Basic game you do not need to  login 
first, just select       1 - Application Programs
                         5 - Basic 


and load the game you want by     run"name" (RETURN)


If you want to write your own program, wish to save or print, you 
should use the user keys to login and load Basic.
Select                   4 - User commands
                         5 - Basic


It  actually loads a file called NBASIC.COM  this file looks down 
the line and loads PNBASIC.COM into Premium Microbees or  SNBASIC 
into Standard Microbees.

At some stage before you attempt to save you should type in
                         diskreset "P" (RETURN)

Now  you can type SAVE "NAME" (RETURN) (With the name you want to 
give the program)

To print out a listing of your program type   LLIST (RETURN)
Then     SYSTEM (RETURN) to move  back to the Workstation Shell
then                     2 - Network functions
                         2 - Print spooled data

If  a  program  prints out as part of its operation  it  will  be 
caught  by  the spool file.   At the completion of the  operation 
just type   SYSTEM (RETURN) to move back to the Workstation Shell
then                     2 - Network functions
                         2 - Print spooled data

and the work will be printed out.
USING SIMPLY WRITE ON THE HARDDISK
----------------------------------

Select the appropriate User Keys to load the program

The Opening Menu screen will appear.

Select ESC 2 and move the cursor down to the line that reads -
CHANGE FILE DRIVE  and press <return>.
     Type      - P <return>

Then select ESC 2 again and move the cursor down to the line that 
reads - CHANGE FONT DRIVE <return>.
     Type      - A: <return>.

Hit the ESC key and the 5 key to go into your document and create 
your docoment as normal.   When you are finished and wish to save 
it hit the ESC key and 1 to return to the Opening menu and ESC  2 
to enter the Disk functions.

Move  the  cursor down using the arrows to SAVE CURRENT FILE  and 
press  <return>  Type in the name you wish to save it  under  and 
again press <return>.

To  print out that document again select ESC 3 the cursor will be 
on the print option so just press <return>.   Again, like all the 
network programs the buffer will catch the file.  

Once  you  have sent your file/files to the printer   you  should 
exit back to the shell by typing an ESC 1

You then select 2 - NETWORK FUNCTIONS
                2 - PRINT

and your work will print out.

COPYING FLOPPY DISKS ACROSS ONTO THE HARDDISK
---------------------------------------------

The  solution  is  to put the floppy into drive E:  and  copy  it 
across from there.

First  you will have to go to the master File Server and go  into 

                         C - Configuration.    
Then  select             M - Miscellaneous Configuration.   

Then make sure the number of  File Server Disks to be  5.
This way it knows that the floppy drive exists.

Now ESC back to          D - Diskette/Workspace Management
                         T - Transfer

You will see the cursor jump to the middle of the screen  waiting 
for you to enter the file name.  You can enter them one at a time 
or have it copy every single file by using the wild cards *.*

The  BACKSPACE  key  will let you move far enough to  change  the 
Drive  letter.   Normally you would want to copy every file  from 
the floppy E drive to the A drive

You would enter         E:*.*   RETURN

                        A:
It will take each file from E one at a time and give it the  same 
name on the A drive.

Now if you later wanted to copy another floppy you will only have 
to tell it that their is a new floppy in by:

selecting -
                         D   Diskette / Work management
                         C   Change Diskette.

Move  the  cursor down to E  (again this means  the  floppy)  and 
press the return key.

The screen will tell you to insert any new disk and press return. 
Once the floppy is in press the return key.

Then you can escape back and repeat the Transfer process.


